const NASA_KNOWLEDGE_DOCS = [{"id":"R","kind":"RIDGE Hazard","title":"Hazard: Space Radiation","feature":"","text":"Space radiation is invisible to the human eye. It’s not only stealthy, but considered one of the most hazardous aspects of human spaceflight.\nWhat is Space Radiation?\nWhy Space Radiation Matters\nSpace Radiation Laboratory\nWhat Is Space Radiation?\nOutside Earth’s protective magnetic field and atmosphere, the ionizing radiation in space will pose a serious ris"},{"id":"I","kind":"RIDGE Hazard","title":"Hazard: Isolation and Confinement","feature":"","text":"Behavioral responses occur among groups of people far from Earth who are isolated and confined in a small space over a long period of time. Crews will be carefully chosen, trained, and supported to ensure they can work effectively as a team for months or years in space.\nOn Earth, we have the luxury of picking up our cell phones and instantly being connected "},{"id":"D","kind":"RIDGE Hazard","title":"Hazard: Distance From Earth","feature":"","text":"Instructions, new supplies, medical care, and more become increasingly challenging to receive from Earth as astronauts venture deeper into space.\nMars is, on average, 140 million miles from Earth. Rather than a three-day lunar trip, astronauts bound for Mars would be leaving our planet for roughly three years.\nGiven this distance, planning and self-sufficien"},{"id":"G","kind":"RIDGE Hazard","title":"Hazard: Gravity Fields","feature":"","text":"Floating weightlessly within a spacecraft can lead to dizziness, bone and muscle loss, vision issues, and more. Astronauts may also need time to adjust to new gravity fields after landing on the Moon or Mars before they conduct mission-critical tasks. Developing strategies that enable crew members to adapt quickly to different gravitational fields will be ke"},{"id":"E","kind":"RIDGE Hazard","title":"Hazard: Hostile/Closed Environments","feature":"","text":"Maintaining a safe ecosystem inside a vehicle presents unique challenges, from ensuring optimal temperatures, pressures, and lighting, to reducing noise, monitoring microbial communities, and tracking immune responses.\nThe ecosystem inside a spacecraft plays a big role in the daily life of an astronaut in space.\nMicrobes can change characteristics in space, "},{"id":"risk_77","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Bone Fracture: Risk of Bone Fracture due to Spaceflight-induced Changes to Bone","feature":"","text":"Given the skeletal changes that occur during spaceflight, there is a possibility that the bones of crewmembers during and after spaceflight are not as strong as they were before spaceflight and a fracture may occur postflight for activities otherwise unlikely to induce fracture prior to space flight. HSRB State of Knowledge Fracture risk (Ops) increases with"},{"id":"risk_78","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"IVD: Concern of Intervertebral Disc Damage upon and immediately after re-exposure to Gravity [inactive]","feature":"","text":"Concern Statement: Given the morphological and possible biochemical changes in the IVD (intervertebral disc) during mechanical unloading in space, there is the possibility of IVD damage upon and immediately after re-exposure to gravity (1/6, 3/8 or 1G). HSRB State of Knowledge The Concern Item does not have a Context (not an HSRB requirement). Mitigation Str"},{"id":"risk_80","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Microhost: Risk of Adverse In-Mission and Long-Term Health Effects and Performance Effects Due to Host-Microorganism Interactions","feature":"","text":"Given that evidence collected during space flight indicates alterations in microbial virulence and astronaut immune function, there is a possibility that infectious disease will have increased prevalence and/or will be more severe during spaceflight missions. HSRB State of Knowledge Current monitoring strategies indicate the presence of opportunistic pathoge"},{"id":"risk_81","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Renal Stone: Risk of Renal Stone Formation","feature":"","text":"Given changes in urinary biochemistry during spaceflight, there is a possibility that symptomatic renal stones will occur, which could cause renal colic (pain), nausea, vomiting, hematuria, infection, and/or hydronephrosis. HSRB State of Knowledge Astronauts are at risk of developing symptomatic renal stones in- and post-flight. Risk mitigation strategies ar"},{"id":"risk_82","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Dust: Risk of Adverse In-Mission Health and Performance Effects and Long-Term Health Effects Due to Celestial Dust Exposure","feature":"","text":"Given the unique properties of lunar and other celestial bodies' dust, there is a possibility that exposure could lead to serious health effects (e.g., respiratory, cardiopulmonary, ocular or dermal harm) or to crew performance impacts during celestial body missions. HSRB State of Knowledge NASA rodent-based research results coupled with expert review has de"},{"id":"risk_84","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"EVA: Risk of Mission Impacting Injury and Compromised Performance and Long-Term Health Effects due to EVA Operations","feature":"","text":"Given the high-performance physiological and functional demands of operating in a self-contained EVA or training suit in various gravity fields and system environments, there is a possibility that crew injury and compromised physiological and functional performance may occur. HSRB State of Knowledge NASA-STD-3001: NASA Spaceflight Human-System Standard Vol 1"},{"id":"risk_85","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Immune: Risk of In Mission Impacts, Adverse Health Events or Long-Term Health Impacts due to Altered Immune Response","feature":"","text":"Given that exposure to orbital spaceflight results in persistent dysregulation of the immune system, which leads to reactivation of latent herpesviruses and likely to an increased clinical incidence, including rashes and hypersensitivity reactions, there is a possibility that crewmembers will be susceptible to certain diseases during exploration class missio"},{"id":"risk_88","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Sensorimotor: Risk of Altered Sensorimotor/Vestibular Function Impacting Critical Mission Tasks, Human Health, and Long-Term Health","feature":"","text":"Given that altered gravity leads to changes in sensorimotor/vestibular function that manifest in motion sickness, spatial disorientation, decrements in postural control and locomotion, and manual and fine motor control deficits, there is a possibility that crew will experience performance decrements in manual/vehicle control, extravehicular activities, and e"},{"id":"risk_95","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Medical Conditions: Risk of Adverse Health Outcomes and Decrements in Performance Due to Medical Conditions that occur in Mission, as well as Long Term Health Outcomes Due to Mission Exposures","feature":"","text":"Given that medical conditions will occur during human spaceflight missions, there is a possibility of adverse health outcomes & decrements in performance during these missions and for long term health. HSRB State of Knowledge Historical spaceflight data have revealed multiple in-flight and post flight medical conditions occur in the astronaut population. Whi"},{"id":"risk_96","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Carcinogenesis: Risk of Radiation Carcinogenesis","feature":"","text":"LxC is the likelihood and consequence rating. The information above was last approved by the Human System Risk Board (HSRB) under the previous 3x4 LxC matrix. The HSRB has now migrated to a new 5x5 LxC matrix as defined in the new Risk Management Plan which may change the risk rating once brought forward for approval. This risk is scheduled to be updated at "},{"id":"risk_99","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Behavioral Health: Risk of Adverse Cognitive or Behavioral Changes and Psychiatric Disorders Leading to In-mission Health and Performance and Long-term Health Effects","feature":"","text":"Given that crews of future exploration missions will be exposed to extended duration of isolation and confinement, greater distances from Earth, as well as increased exposures to radiation and altered gravity, there is a possibility that these singular or combined hazards could lead to (a) adverse cognitive or behavioral changes affecting crew health and per"},{"id":"risk_100","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Sleep: Risk of Performance Decrements and Adverse Health Outcomes Resulting from Sleep Loss, Circadian Desynchronization, and Work Overload","feature":"","text":"Given that astronauts experience sleep loss, circadian desynchronization, and work overload, there is a possibility that performance decrements and adverse health outcomes will occur. HSRB State of Knowledge There is evidence of sleep deficiency inflight as crews sleep 6 h/night and increase their sleep and circadian misalignment. There is also evidence of w"},{"id":"risk_101","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Team: Risk of In-Mission Performance and Behavioral Health Decrements Due to Inadequate Cooperation, Coordination, Communication, and Psychosocial Adaptation within a Team","feature":"","text":"Given that the conditions of space missions may lead to inadequate functioning within a team (inadequate cooperation, coordination, communication and/or psychosocial adaptation), which includes flight crew and ground support, there is a possibility that performance and behavioral health decrements will occur. HSRB State of Knowledge There is evidence of occa"},{"id":"risk_105","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"SANS: Risk of Spaceflight Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome (SANS)","feature":"","text":"Given that ocular and brain structural changes develop during spaceflight, there is a possibility that these changes could lead to short-term or long-term vision alterations, cognitive effects, or other deleterious health effects. HSRB State of Knowledge Spaceflight Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome (SANS) is a syndrome unique to humans that fly in space, and"},{"id":"risk_167","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Dynamic Loads: Risk of In-Mission Injury and Performance Decrements and Long-term Health Effects due to Dynamic Loads","feature":"","text":"Given the exposure to the range of anticipated dynamic loads that might be applied to crew within the hostile closed environment of a spacecraft and/or surface vehicle (suited or unsuited) could result in injury to the crew, resulting in a possibility that mission task performance would be impaired, or tasks could not be performed. HSRB State of Knowledge St"},{"id":"risk_168","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"DCS: Risk of Mission Impacts and Long-Term Health Issues due to Decompression Sickness","feature":"","text":"Given that decompression sickness (DCS) is a potential risk for every spaceflight/mission (e.g., EVA or cabin depressurization) and must be mitigated to acceptable risk levels; limitations in quantitative understanding of DCS will lead to conservative DCS risk mitigation countermeasures with significant operational, engineering, and/or secondary health/perfo"},{"id":"risk_173","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Hypoxia: Risk of Reduced Crew Health and Performance Due to Hypoxia","feature":"","text":"Given that future human exploration missions require robust, flexible Extravehicular Activity (EVA) architecture protocols that include the use of a reduced pressure cabin atmosphere enabling staged denitrogenation, there is a possibility that this atmosphere could result in compromised health and performance to the crewmember due to exposure to mild hypobar"},{"id":"risk_175","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"EIHSO: Risk of Adverse Mission Outcomes Due to Earth Independent Human-Systems Operations","feature":"","text":"Given decreasing real-time ground support for execution of Earth independent critical, complex operations during future exploration missions, there is a possibility of adverse performance outcomes, most consequentially that crew are unable to adequately respond to unanticipated critical malfunctions or execute safety-critical procedures. HSRB State of Knowle"},{"id":"risk_176","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Food and Nutrition: Risk of Performance Decrement and Crew Illness Due to Inadequate Food and Nutrition","feature":"","text":"Given that there is a constrained ability to supply adequate food in spaceflight missions, there is a possibility that inadequate nutrition will result in performance decrement, crew illness, and long-term health effects. HSRB State of Knowledge Much is known about the basic nutrition required to maintain performance and health during spaceflight. Standards/"},{"id":"risk_177","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Pharm: Risk of Ineffective or Toxic Medications During Long-Duration Exploration Spaceflight","feature":"","text":"Given that there is no current method to sufficiently characterize medication use, drug quality and performance, clinical outcomes, and the impact of a hostile space environment on pharmaceutical stability and potency during long-duration exploration missions, there is a possibility that provision of a safe and effective drug treatment will be significantly "},{"id":"risk_179","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Cardiovascular: Risk of Cardiovascular Adaptations Contributing to Adverse Mission Performance and Health Outcomes","feature":"","text":"Given exposure to the spaceflight environment can contribute to cardiovascular deconditioning, dysfunction, and remodeling, there is a possibility that crews will experience impaired performance and adverse cardiovascular health outcomes that necessitate intervention during and after spaceflight and planetary operations. HSRB State of Knowledge Exposure to w"},{"id":"risk_180","kind":"NASA HRP Risk","title":"Muscle and Aerobic: Risk of Mission Performance Decrements and Adverse Health Outcomes Resulting from Reduced Cardiorespiratory and Musculoskeletal Fitness","feature":"","text":"Given that exposure to a reduced gravity environment causes cardio-respiratory (e.g., maximal oxygen consumption and ventilatory threshold) and musculoskeletal fitness (e.g., strength, power, and endurance) to decline, there is a risk of impaired mission task performance and adverse health outcomes. Note: This risk is carried as two separate risks at the HSR"},{"id":"BLOOD_GLUCOSE","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Blood Glucose (Fasting / Pre-Post Exercise)","feature":"blood_glucose","text":"Nominal: 70 - 99 mg/dL. Unloading of postural muscles (soleus/quadriceps) reduces GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake and induces spaceflight-associated insulin resistance. Finger-prick micro-glucometer or wearable continuous interstitial glucose sensor; verified via Inspiration4 CMP (OSD-575). Countermeasure: Daily high-intensity ergometer + ARED resistance workouts; balanced low-glycemic spaceflight nutrition; hydration "},{"id":"BLOOD_PRESSURE","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure (Systolic / Diastolic)","feature":"systolic_bp,diastolic_bp","text":"Nominal: 95-120 / 60-80 mmHg. Cephalad fluid shift (1.5-2 L from legs to thorax/head) raises upper-body vascular pressure in orbit and causes post-flight orthostatic intolerance. Oscillometric arm cuff held strictly at heart level while stationary for 60s; Doppler ultrasound for jugular flow. Countermeasure: Lower Body Negative Pressure (LBNP) device, fluid-loading electrolyte protocol before re-entry, aerobic cyclin"},{"id":"HEART_RATE","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Resting Heart Rate","feature":"heart_rate_bpm","text":"Nominal: 55 - 90 bpm. Cardiac remodeling, reduced plasma volume (10-15%), and altered autonomic baroreflex sensitivity increase arrhythmia/tachycardia risk. 12-lead ECG, chest-strap telemetry, or optical PPG wearable; linked to radiation cardiac studies (OSD-435). Countermeasure: Daily cardiovascular ergometer conditioning, electrolyte balance, 12-lead ECG uplink during chest pain/palpitations."},{"id":"CORE_TEMP","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Core Body Temperature","feature":"body_temp","text":"Nominal: 36.2 - 37.3 °C. Absence of convective cooling and sweat pooling causes 'Space Fever' (+0.5 to +1.0°C baseline elevation during exercise/rest). Temporal artery forehead thermometer or ingestible telemetry pill. Countermeasure: Increase cabin airflow fans, liquid-cooling ventilation garment (LCVG) during EVA, check infection/latent viral reactivation."},{"id":"SPO2","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Blood Oxygen Saturation (SpO2)","feature":"spo2_pct","text":"Nominal: 95 - 100 %. Mild hypoxemia risks during EVA prebreathe, decompression events, or Lunar/Mars reduced-pressure habitat atmospheres (8.2 psi / 34% O2). Fingertip pulse oximetry + cabin PPO2/CO2 ECLSS telemetry. Countermeasure: Immediate supplemental O2 (4 L/min), verify cabin O2/CO2 scrubbers, screen for decompression sickness (DCS) symptoms."},{"id":"SLEEP_DURATION","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Daily Sleep Duration & Circadian Alignment","feature":"sleep_hours","text":"Nominal: 6.5 - 8.5 hours/day. 16 orbital sunrises/sunsets per 24h, cabin noise, CO2 pockets, and workload shifts cause chronic sleep debt and circadian desynchronization. Wrist actigraphy, sleep logs, and PVT cognitive reaction testing. Countermeasure: Blue-enriched morning / amber evening solid-state lighting, sleep-station airflow fans, CBT-I & scheduled wind-down."},{"id":"BEHAVIORAL_MOOD","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Behavioral Health & Mood Score (PHQ-9 / Stress Scale)","feature":"mood_score","text":"Nominal: 1 1-4 scale (1=Optimal, 4=Severe). Prolonged isolation, confinement, crew friction, and communication delay (>22 min one-way to Mars) elevate anxiety, depression, and cognitive fatigue. Self-report mood check-ins, speech/facial affect biomarkers, and computerized cognitive batteries. Countermeasure: Private family audio/video conferences, autonomous psychological support tools, workload balancing, structured"},{"id":"SENSORIMOTOR_BALANCE","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Postural Stability & Sensorimotor Balance Score","feature":"balance_score","text":"Nominal: 82 - 100 % (0-100). Otolith unloading and proprioceptive reweighting cause space motion sickness in orbit and severe postural ataxia upon gravity transitions (Moon/Mars/Earth). Computerized MediaPipe Pose sway kinematics, tandem walk, and sit-to-stand balance tests. Countermeasure: Vestibular adaptation exercises, handrail anchoring, Promethazine for acute SMS, sensorimotor reconditioning before EVA."},{"id":"JOINT_SYMMETRY","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Bilateral Joint Kinematics Symmetry Difference","feature":"joint_symmetry_diff","text":"Nominal: 0.0 - 4.5 degrees. Asymmetric musculoskeletal deconditioning, EVA suit shoulder/hand strain, or intervertebral disc swelling (spinal elongation) causes kinematic asymmetry. Real-time pose landmark joint angle symmetry (left vs right shoulder, hip, knee) via onboard camera. Countermeasure: Balanced ARED resistance loading, spinal decompression stretching, EVA suit fit verification."},{"id":"OCULAR_EAR_SANS","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Ocular Fatigue (EAR) & SANS Neuro-Ocular Status","feature":"facial_ear","text":"Nominal: EAR >= 0.235 | IOP 10-21 mmHg EAR ratio / IOP mmHg. Headward fluid shift elevates intracranial/choroidal pressure, causing optic disc edema, globe flattening, choroidal folds, and hyperopic shift (SANS). FaceMesh Eye Aspect Ratio (EAR) for fatigue + In-flight OCT, Fundoscopy, Tonometry IOP, and ocular ultrasound (OSD-583/679). Countermeasure: LBNP fluid redistribution, B-vitamin/one-carbon metabolic suppleme"},{"id":"RADIATION_EXPOSURE","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Space Radiation Dose Rate & Cumulative Exposure (GCR / SPE / Neutrons)","feature":"radiation_dose_msv","text":"Nominal: < 1.0 mSv/day (LEO ~0.4-0.8 mSv/day) mSv / day (or mSv cumulative). GCR high-Z/energy ions and secondary neutrons (~30% of ISS dose per CSA CANS) damage DNA, elevate lifetime cancer risk, and injure CNS/cardiovascular tissue. Active dosimeters (RAD), CSA Canadian Active Neutron Spectrometer (CANS), Radi-N2 bubble detectors, crew personal badges. Countermeasure: Retreat to Aft Water Wall / polyethylene storm "},{"id":"CABIN_CO2","kind":"Clinical Indicator","title":"Cabin CO2 Partial Pressure (ppCO2)","feature":"cabin_co2_mmhg","text":"Nominal: < 2.0 mmHg. Closed ECLSS habitats accumulate CO2 pockets around crew without airflow, causing headaches, cognitive impairment, hypercapnia, and exacerbating SANS. Continuous ECLSS CO2 infrared analyzers + transcutaneous pCO2 monitoring. Countermeasure: Verify CDRA/amine scrubber status, maximize sleep-station ventilation fans, inspect filters for lunar/martian dust."},{"id":"chest-pain","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: Coronary & Angina Triage","feature":"","text":"Trigger: Acute chest pain, resting HR > 120 bpm, SBP > 180 or < 85 mmHg. Actions: Halt all physical exercise (ergometer/ARED) immediately and secure crew member in sleep station harness. Deploy Clinical Medical Pack Alpha: Initiate 12-lead ECG telemetry stream to Flight Surgeon console. Administer supplemental oxygen via oral-nasal mask at 4 L/min. Verify astronaut has not cons"},{"id":"dizziness","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: Presyncope & Orthostatic Instability","feature":"","text":"Trigger: Presyncope, SBP < 90 mmHg, post-landing/gravity transition dizziness. Actions: Secure astronaut in supine position with feet elevated to alleviate cephalic hypoperfusion. Administer 1.0 L oral electrolyte rehydration solution within 60 minutes. Check continuous blood pressure cuff every 5 minutes for postural hypotension. Verify cabin CO2 levels on ECLSS sensor (ensure"},{"id":"balance-loss","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: Vestibular Ataxia & Otolith Decompensation","feature":"","text":"Trigger: Balance score < 62%, joint asymmetry > 13°, acute vertigo/nausea. Actions: Anchor crew member to primary handrails to prevent rotational tumble and head impact. Restrict rapid head movements in pitch and yaw axes (mitigate Coriolis effect). Administer Promethazine 25mg IM or oral if accompanied by active space motion sickness (SMS). Inspect cabin air intake filters for"},{"id":"radiation","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: Solar Particle Event (SPE) Alert","feature":"","text":"Trigger: SPE flux > 50 mSv/hr or CANS/RAD dosimeter surge alert. Actions: Immediately sound habitat klaxon: all crew retreat to Aft Water Wall / polyethylene storm shelter. Terminate ongoing Extravehicular Activities (EVA) within 8-minute airlock contingency window. Record active personal dosimeter (RAD) and CSA CANS neutron spectrometer readings immediately. Deploy auxiliary p"},{"id":"hypoxia-dcs","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: Hypoxia / Decompression Sickness (DCS) Triage","feature":"","text":"Trigger: SpO2 < 90%, cabin depressurization, joint pain ('bends') post-EVA. Actions: Don emergency 100% O2 mask or seal EVA suit pressure loop immediately. Verify cabin total pressure, PPO2, and ppCO2 on ECLSS console. If DCS suspected post-EVA, initiate hyperbaric treatment protocol (suit/airlock repressurization + 100% O2). Log neurological exam, pulse oximetry, and vital sig"},{"id":"sans-vision","kind":"Emergency Protocol","title":"Acute Protocol: SANS Visual Acuity & Intracranial Pressure Shift","feature":"","text":"Trigger: Acute hyperopic shift, scotoma, IOP > 22 mmHg, or sustained EAR < 0.195. Actions: Perform in-flight Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT), Fundoscopy, and Tonometry IOP check. Measure visual acuity using onboard Amsler grid and Space Anticipation Glasses calibration. Check cabin ppCO2 (< 2.0 mmHg target, as elevated CO2 worsens cerebral vasodilation). Schedule LBNP sessio"},{"id":"1","kind":"CSA Mission Data","title":"Why do we conduct science experiments in space?","feature":"","text":"Science is our quest for more knowledge about the world and ourselves. For thousands of years, humanity has pushed boundaries in its search to learn more about our place in the universe.\nCanadian science on the International Space Station\nCanada's participation in the International Space Station ( ISS ) allows our scientists to access the unique space enviro"},{"id":"2","kind":"CSA Mission Data","title":"How does space affect the human body? – Infographic","feature":"","text":"All - Show images and videos\nImages - Show images only\nVideos - Show videos only\nSpace has tremendous effects on the human body! As we prepare for journeys to more distant destinations like Mars, humankind must tackle these risks to ensure safe travel for our modern explorers. The impacts of microgravity mirror aging and the complications of a sedentary life"},{"id":"3","kind":"CSA Mission Data","title":"Monitoring radiation for astronaut health","feature":"","text":"Canadian Space Agency ( CSA ) astronaut David Saint-Jacques using bubble detectors, a predecessor to Canadian Active Neutron Spectrometer (CANS), on the International Space Station ( ISS ). (Credits: CSA / NASA )\nRadiation is one of the key challenges faced by astronauts living and working beyond the protective shield of Earth’s atmosphere. As Canada and its"}];
